.TH LSVMBUS 1 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lsvmbus \- list Hyper-V VMBus devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsvmbus
.RB [ \-v ]...
.br
.B lsvmbus
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsvmbus
lists the devices on the Hyper-V VMBus, as the kernel presents them in
.IR \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:devices/ .
Devices are sorted by VMBus ID, which is the relative ID
.RI ( child_relid )
of the device's primary channel.
.PP
Without options, each device is shown on one line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
VMBUS ID \fIid\fP: \fIdescription\fP
.EE
.RE
.PP
The description is looked up from the device's class ID in a table of
Hyper-V device types built into the program, for example
.B "Synthetic \%network adapter"
or
.BR [Heartbeat] .
Devices whose class ID is not in the table are described as
.BR Unknown .
.PP
Non-option arguments are ignored.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show more detail.
Given once, the output adds the device's class ID and, on following
lines, the mapping of each of the device's channels to a CPU, in the
form
.BI "Rel_ID=" relid ", target_cpu=" cpu\fR,
sorted by channel relative ID.
Given two or more times, the output also includes the device's instance
ID
.RB ( Device_ID )
and its sysfs path.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary to standard output and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid option.
.TP
.B 255
.I \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:devices
does not exist; a message saying so is printed on standard output.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:devices/\:* /id
VMBus ID: the relative ID of the device's primary channel.
.TP
.IR \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:devices/\:* /class_id
Class ID (interface type GUID) of the device.
.TP
.IR \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:devices/\:* /device_id
Device ID (interface instance GUID) of the device.
.TP
.IR \%/sys/\:bus/\:vmbus/\:devices/\:* /channel_vp_mapping
Mapping of the device's channels to CPUs.
.SH NOTES
.B lsvmbus
is a Python program and runs under
.BR python3 .
.PP
The VMBus directory in sysfs exists only when the kernel's VMBus
driver
.RB \%( CONFIG_HYPERV_VMBUS )
is active, that is, in a Linux guest running on Hyper-V.
.SH EXAMPLES
List every device with its class ID, instance ID, sysfs path and
channel-to-CPU mapping:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ lsvmbus \-vv
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR \%hv_fcopy_uio_daemon (8),
.BR \%hv_kvp_daemon (8),
.BR \%hv_vss_daemon (8)
.PP
.I \%Documentation/\:ABI/\:stable/\:sysfs\-bus\-vmbus
and
.I \%Documentation/\:virt/\:hyperv/\:vmbus.rst
in the Linux source.
